window.HIST = {"topic":{"subject":"history","course":"1ESO","topic":"prehistory","title":"Prehistory (part 2): Neolithic, Age of Metals and Megalithism","language":"en","source":{"document":"UNIT PREHISTORY 2.docx.pdf","pages":18,"notes":"Teacher's notes (Google Docs). Every page has text except page 18 (only an image: a periodic table, referenced from pages 13-14). Images with text were read (Harari comic on pages 4-7). YouTube/press links are not content.","page_coverage":{"1":"neolithic-revolution","2":"neolithic-revolution (examples of revolution, activity) + advantages-of-agriculture","3":"advantages-of-agriculture + disadvantage-diseases","4":"disadvantage-diseases + domesticated-species (comic + heading, mostly images)","5":"domesticated-species (comic with dates) + harari","6":"harari (most successful species)","7":"harari (comic) + food-shortages + activity 'Explica estas imágenes'","8":"food-shortages (images) + social-hierarchies","9":"barter + religion","10":"religion + new-tools + ceramics","11":"ceramics + basketry-and-textiles","12":"where-neolithic + age-of-metals (timeline)","13":"metals (copper, bronze)","14":"metals (iron) + megalithism","15":"megaliths: menhir","16":"megaliths: dolmen","17":"megaliths: cromlech","18":"image only: periodic table (no new content)"},"activities_in_document":[{"page":2,"what":"Fill in the blanks: examples of revolution (the document leaves the names blank)"},{"page":4,"what":"Learn by heart at least 7 domesticated species"},{"page":7,"what":"Explica estas imágenes (photo of an indigenous tribe vs. photo of businessmen)"},{"page":11,"what":"Fill in: 'Típico _______ asturiano o gallego' (answer given: HÓRREO)"},{"page":12,"what":"Draw a timetable (timeline of the Age of Metals)"}]},"sections":[{"id":"neolithic-revolution","title":"Paleolithic vs Neolithic: the Neolithic Revolution","pages":[1,2],"points":["The difference between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic is agriculture and livestock farming.","About 10,000 years ago, Homo sapiens sapiens discovered they could tame animals (livestock farming) and cultivate plants (agriculture).","Until then, the period is called the PALEOLITHIC: cave life, nomadic, hunting and gathering. Paleolithic means \"old stone\".","From this point on, it is called the NEOLITHIC (\"new stone\").","For a long time, historians have talked about the Neolithic Revolution.","A revolution is a change that transforms the world (\"when something changes radically\")."],"lists":{"examples_of_revolution":[{"clue":"_____ Revolution 1789","answer":"French","added":true},{"clue":"_____ Revolution: in the 19th century, factories with machines began to appear. Until then, everything was made by hand (handicraft).","answer":"Industrial","added":true},{"clue":"_____ Revolution in 1917: it turned Russia into the USSR, a communist country.","answer":"Russian","added":true},{"clue":"_____ Revolution","answer":"Neolithic","added":true}]},"notes":"The four revolution names are blank in the document (class activity); the answers are added by us. The 4th blank is most likely the Neolithic Revolution (the topic of the page).","vocabulary":[{"en":"Paleolithic","es":"Paleolítico (edad de la piedra antigua)"},{"en":"Neolithic","es":"Neolítico (edad de la piedra nueva)"},{"en":"agriculture","es":"agricultura"},{"en":"livestock farming","es":"ganadería"},{"en":"to tame","es":"domesticar / amansar","added":true},{"en":"to cultivate","es":"cultivar","added":true},{"en":"nomadic","es":"nómada"},{"en":"hunting and gathering","es":"caza y recolección"},{"en":"revolution","es":"revolución"},{"en":"handicraft","es":"artesanía (hecho a mano)","added":true}],"dates":[{"when":"about 10,000 years ago","what":"Homo sapiens sapiens starts agriculture and livestock farming: beginning of the Neolithic"},{"when":"1789","what":"(French) Revolution"},{"when":"19th century","what":"(Industrial) Revolution: factories with machines"},{"when":"1917","what":"(Russian) Revolution: Russia becomes the USSR, a communist country"}],"footnotes":["Neo is \"new\" in Latin (as written in the document); neologismo = palabra nueva, p. ej. selfie, wifi."]},{"id":"advantages-of-agriculture","title":"Advantages of agriculture","pages":[2,3],"points":["1. It allows people to be sedentary, and towns and cities are built.","Sedentary is the opposite of nomadic.","Example: Çatalhöyük (Catal Huyuk), in present-day Turkey.","The first cities were built in an area called the Fertile Crescent: a territory with the first villages with agriculture and livestock farming.","2. It allows humans to grow their own food.","3. It leads to an increase in population, resulting in more babies: more food = more births."],"lists":{"fertile_crescent_countries_today":["Turkey","Palestine","Israel","Iraq","Syria","Lebanon"]},"vocabulary":[{"en":"sedentary","es":"sedentario"},{"en":"Fertile Crescent","es":"Creciente Fértil"},{"en":"town / city","es":"pueblo / ciudad","added":true},{"en":"population","es":"población"},{"en":"birth","es":"nacimiento","added":true}],"dates":[{"when":"7500 BC","what":"Map of the Fertile Crescent (date shown on the map image)"}]},{"id":"disadvantage-diseases","title":"A very interesting fact and disadvantage: diseases","pages":[3,4],"points":["More people = more contagion.","Humans now coexist with animals, and some animal diseases are transmitted to humans.","Examples: COVID (bats?), rabies (transmitted by infected dogs), AIDS.","When humans were nomadic and had not yet domesticated animals, they did not have these diseases."],"notes":"In the document the sentence 'It reduces the incidence of diseases' is crossed out: it is the WRONG idea. The page also shows a Benetton AIDS advert and a press clipping (images only).","vocabulary":[{"en":"disadvantage","es":"desventaja","added":true},{"en":"contagion","es":"contagio"},{"en":"disease","es":"enfermedad","added":true},{"en":"rabies","es":"rabia"},{"en":"AIDS","es":"SIDA"},{"en":"bat","es":"murciélago","added":true}]},{"id":"domesticated-species","title":"Domesticated species (learn at least 7 by heart)","pages":[4,5],"points":["Of the thousands of species that ancient humans hunted and gathered, only a few were domesticable.","These few species lived in certain places, and it was there that the agricultural revolutions happened.","Even today, half of the calories that feed humans come from only three plants (wheat, rice and corn) and two animals (cows and pigs).","All of them were domesticated more than 9,000 years ago!"],"notes":"The points are translated from the Spanish speech bubbles of the Harari comic (Sapiens, graphic novel) shown as images on pages 4-5. Dates are years before Christ as written in the comic (−10,000 = 10,000 BC). Translations into English are ours.","lists":{"species_with_dates":[{"en":"wheat","es":"trigo","when":"12,000 BC"},{"en":"peas and lentils","es":"guisantes y lentejas","when":"10,000 BC"},{"en":"goat","es":"cabra","when":"10,000 BC"},{"en":"sheep","es":"oveja","when":"10,500 BC"},{"en":"pig","es":"cerdo","when":"10,500 BC"},{"en":"cow","es":"vaca","when":"10,500 BC"},{"en":"zebu","es":"cebú","when":"8,500 BC"},{"en":"hen / chicken","es":"gallina","when":"8,000 BC"},{"en":"cat","es":"gato","when":"8,000 BC"},{"en":"pistachio","es":"pistacho","when":"8,000 BC"},{"en":"grapevine","es":"parra","when":"8,000 BC"},{"en":"olive tree","es":"olivo","when":"6,500 BC"},{"en":"horse","es":"caballo","when":"5,500 BC"},{"en":"camel","es":"camello","when":"5,000 BC"},{"en":"llama","es":"llama","when":"5,000 BC"}],"not_domesticated_in_comic":["bramble (zarzas)","bear","gorilla","mammoth","lion","Komodo dragon / lizard"]},"vocabulary":[{"en":"to domesticate","es":"domesticar"},{"en":"species","es":"especie","added":true},{"en":"wheat","es":"trigo"},{"en":"goat","es":"cabra"},{"en":"sheep","es":"oveja"},{"en":"horse","es":"caballo"},{"en":"olive tree","es":"olivo"},{"en":"grapevine","es":"parra"}]},{"id":"harari","title":"Was agriculture a great advance? Yuval Noah Harari","pages":[5,6,7],"points":["Contrary to what has been thought for centuries (that agriculture was a great human advance), some anthropologists today believe it was NOT.","Yuval Noah Harari, author of the book \"Sapiens\".","According to Harari, the most successful domesticated / cultivated species are: 1. wheat, 2. corn (American), 3. sugar cane (Asian), 4. rice (Asia), 5. potato (American).","Today, wheat would cover all of Western Europe (more than 2 million km² of farmland).","The potato was brought to Europe by the Spanish after Columbus.","Maybe humans lived better before agriculture, when they hunted and gathered!"],"lists":{"most_successful_species":[{"rank":1,"en":"wheat","es":"trigo","origin":null},{"rank":2,"en":"corn","es":"maíz","origin":"American"},{"rank":3,"en":"sugar cane","es":"caña de azúcar","origin":"Asian"},{"rank":4,"en":"rice","es":"arroz","origin":"Asia"},{"rank":5,"en":"potato","es":"patata","origin":"American"}]},"comic_page_7":["Hunter-gatherers led a pleasant life hunting and gathering.","But to control wheat they had to put all their effort into it.","A few thousand years later: looking after the crop from morning to night.","\"The pact your ancestor made was a total swindle!\" — \"The life of a gatherer could be hard, demanding and dangerous… but my life as a farmer is much worse!\""],"notes":"comic_page_7 and the 2 million km² figure are our translations of the comic's Spanish speech bubbles (pages 6-7). The comic on page 4 says: 'From sunrise to sunset he sowed the seeds, watered the plants, pulled up weeds, harvested and ground the grain… more and more, without rest!'","vocabulary":[{"en":"anthropologist","es":"antropólogo","added":true},{"en":"corn","es":"maíz"},{"en":"sugar cane","es":"caña de azúcar"},{"en":"rice","es":"arroz","added":true},{"en":"potato","es":"patata","added":true},{"en":"to gather","es":"recolectar"}],"dates":[{"when":"after Columbus (1492)","what":"the Spanish bring the potato to Europe","added_detail":"1492 is added"}]},{"id":"food-shortages","title":"Food shortages","pages":[7,8],"points":["With larger human populations, there can sometimes be food shortages.","Why? Because at that time agriculture depended completely on the climate: if there were floods, drought or plagues (of insects), crops were lost.","Examples shown: locust plagues in Africa, in La Serena (Badajoz) and in ancient Egypt (according to the Bible)."],"vocabulary":[{"en":"food shortage","es":"escasez de alimentos"},{"en":"climate","es":"clima","added":true},{"en":"flood","es":"inundación"},{"en":"drought","es":"sequía"},{"en":"plague","es":"plaga (de insectos)"},{"en":"crop","es":"cosecha / cultivo","added":true},{"en":"locust","es":"langosta","added":true}]},{"id":"social-hierarchies","title":"First cities and the first social hierarchies","pages":[8],"points":["With agriculture, the first cities and the first SOCIAL HIERARCHIES emerge.","Hierarchy (jerarquía): gradation of people, values or dignities.","How do the first kings and elites arise? By force.","And because kings and elites have the best lands: if you have the best lands (near the river), you produce more, sell more, obtain more and have more power."],"lists":{"social_pyramid_top_to_bottom":["Kings and Nobility","Priests","Scribes","Craftsmen and Peasants","Slaves"]},"notes":"The social pyramid is an image in the document (page 8).","vocabulary":[{"en":"social hierarchy","es":"jerarquía social"},{"en":"king","es":"rey","added":true},{"en":"elite","es":"élite","added":true},{"en":"nobility","es":"nobleza","added":true},{"en":"priest","es":"sacerdote","added":true},{"en":"scribe","es":"escriba","added":true},{"en":"craftsman","es":"artesano","added":true},{"en":"peasant","es":"campesino","added":true},{"en":"slave","es":"esclavo","added":true},{"en":"power","es":"poder","added":true}]},{"id":"barter","title":"The barter system","pages":[9],"points":["With agriculture and livestock farming, the BARTER system is born.","Luxury products, such as votive axes made of variscite, are exchanged."],"vocabulary":[{"en":"barter","es":"trueque"},{"en":"luxury product","es":"producto de lujo","added":true},{"en":"votive","es":"votivo (ofrecido por voto o promesa)"},{"en":"axe","es":"hacha","added":true},{"en":"variscite","es":"variscita (piedra verde)","added":true},{"en":"to exchange","es":"intercambiar","added":true}]},{"id":"religion","title":"Religion is born","pages":[9,10],"points":["Also, in this time period, RELIGION is born.","The Venus of Willendorf: maybe it is a goddess of fertility.","The first religions are very primitive (fire, water, earth, wind).","These religions are called ANIMIST.","Example shown: Navajo rain dance (Native American)."],"vocabulary":[{"en":"religion","es":"religión"},{"en":"goddess of fertility","es":"diosa de la fertilidad"},{"en":"animist","es":"animista"},{"en":"rain dance","es":"danza de la lluvia","added":true}]},{"id":"new-tools","title":"New tools","pages":[10],"points":["New tools emerge: sickle, axes, mill / mortar, hoe."],"lists":{"tools":[{"en":"sickle","es":"hoz"},{"en":"axe","es":"hacha","added":true},{"en":"mill / mortar","es":"molino / mortero"},{"en":"hoe","es":"azada"}]},"vocabulary":[{"en":"tool","es":"herramienta","added":true},{"en":"sickle","es":"hoz"},{"en":"mortar","es":"mortero"},{"en":"hoe","es":"azada"}]},{"id":"ceramics","title":"Ceramics (pottery) are invented","pages":[10,11],"points":["CERAMICS (pottery) are invented. Why?","1. To preserve food, because now there start to be surpluses.","2. Because it is a way to avoid mice and plagues (example: the hórreo, typical of Asturias and Galicia).","3. For transporting things."],"vocabulary":[{"en":"ceramics / pottery","es":"cerámica"},{"en":"to preserve","es":"conservar","added":true},{"en":"surplus","es":"excedente"},{"en":"mouse / mice","es":"ratón / ratones","added":true},{"en":"hórreo","es":"hórreo (granero elevado asturiano o gallego)"}]},{"id":"basketry-and-textiles","title":"Basketry, the loom and the spindle","pages":[11],"points":["BASKETRY is also invented.","The LOOM and the SPINDLE are also invented.","The spindle is an object used to spin textile fibres; it dates from the Neolithic and is still used today (caption in the image, in Spanish)."],"vocabulary":[{"en":"basketry","es":"cestería"},{"en":"loom","es":"telar"},{"en":"spindle","es":"huso"}]},{"id":"where-neolithic","title":"Where does the Neolithic appear?","pages":[12],"points":["In the Fertile Crescent.","In Eastern Asia, in some valleys of important rivers, such as the Indus or the Huang He (Yellow River).","In America: in the central region (the Maya and Aztecs, many centuries later) and the Andean region (the Incas, many centuries later)."],"lists":{"map_regions":["Mesopotamia","Nile Valley (Egypt)","Indus River Region (Harappa)","Huang Ho River Region (Shang, etc.)","Mesoamerica (Mayas, Aztecs, etc.)","Andean Region (Incas, etc.)"]},"notes":"map_regions are the labels of the world map image on page 12.","vocabulary":[{"en":"valley","es":"valle","added":true},{"en":"river","es":"río","added":true},{"en":"Yellow River (Huang He)","es":"río Amarillo"},{"en":"Andean region","es":"zona de los Andes"}]},{"id":"age-of-metals","title":"The Age of Metals","pages":[12],"points":["With metallurgy (the art of making metals), which appeared in the 7th millennium in the Near East (Palestine, Syria, Iraq, etc.), a new era was born.","Timeline: Copper Age 5,000 – Bronze Age 3,000 – Iron Age 2,000."],"notes":"The document gives the timeline numbers without 'BC'; they are years before Christ (added interpretation). The 7th millennium is also BC. Activity: draw a timeline.","vocabulary":[{"en":"metallurgy","es":"metalurgia"},{"en":"Near East","es":"Próximo Oriente"},{"en":"timeline","es":"eje cronológico"},{"en":"millennium","es":"milenio","added":true},{"en":"Copper Age","es":"Edad del Cobre","added":true},{"en":"Bronze Age","es":"Edad del Bronce","added":true},{"en":"Iron Age","es":"Edad del Hierro","added":true}],"dates":[{"when":"7th millennium (BC)","what":"metallurgy appears in the Near East"},{"when":"5,000 (BC)","what":"Copper Age"},{"when":"3,000 (BC)","what":"Bronze Age"},{"when":"2,000 (BC)","what":"Iron Age"}]},{"id":"metals","title":"Copper, bronze and iron","pages":[13,14],"points":["1. Copper (Cu, 29 in the periodic table): a good conductor of electricity. It is used in wires (and is often stolen today). Its main problem: it is not very hard.","2. Bronze: an ALLOY of COPPER and TIN (Sn, 50). It is harder than copper. Tin is another metal.","All the weapons in the books the Iliad and the Odyssey are made of bronze.","3. Iron (Fe, 26 in the periodic table): harder and even more resistant than bronze.","Ferrum is Latin for iron. Spanish words derived from ferrum: ferrocarril, ferretería, férreo."],"lists":{"metals_in_order":[{"en":"copper","es":"cobre","symbol":"Cu","number":29,"fact":"good conductor of electricity; not very hard"},{"en":"bronze","es":"bronce","symbol":null,"number":null,"fact":"alloy of copper and tin; harder than copper"},{"en":"iron","es":"hierro","symbol":"Fe","number":26,"fact":"harder and more resistant than bronze"}]},"notes":"The document says 'Sn 50' and 'Fe 26' (atomic numbers). Page 18 is a periodic table image for reference.","vocabulary":[{"en":"copper","es":"cobre"},{"en":"tin","es":"estaño"},{"en":"bronze","es":"bronce","added":true},{"en":"iron","es":"hierro","added":true},{"en":"alloy","es":"aleación"},{"en":"conductor of electricity","es":"conductor de la electricidad","added":true},{"en":"wire","es":"cable","added":true},{"en":"hard","es":"duro","added":true},{"en":"weapon","es":"arma","added":true}]},{"id":"megalithism","title":"Megalithism","pages":[14,15,16,17],"points":["MEGALITHISM (5th to 2nd millennium) is a prehistoric cultural and architectural phenomenon characterized by the construction of monumental structures using large, unworked stones.","MEGA = big, LITHISM = stone.","There are three types of megaliths: menhir, dolmen and cromlech.","1. Menhir: we don't know for sure what it was for. Maybe to mark a territory?","Example: the Grand Menhir Brisé d'Er Grah, in a row of 19 giant menhirs, erected c. 4500 BC in Brittany, France (image caption).","2. Dolmen: these were tombs.","In Spain, the Dolmen de Menga stands out, in Antequera (province of Málaga), from the 4th millennium.","3. Cromlech: megaliths in the form of a circle. It has to do with some solar or agricultural cult.","The most famous cromlech is Stonehenge."],"lists":{"megalith_types":[{"en":"menhir","what":"a single standing stone; purpose unknown, maybe to mark a territory"},{"en":"dolmen","what":"a tomb","example":"Dolmen de Menga (Antequera, Málaga)"},{"en":"cromlech","what":"megaliths in a circle; solar or agricultural cult","example":"Stonehenge"}],"dolmen_parts_from_diagram":[{"es":"losa de cubierta","en":"capstone","added":true},{"es":"ortostatos o losas verticales","en":"upright stones (orthostats)","added":true},{"es":"corredor interior","en":"inner corridor","added":true},{"es":"túmulo o colina","en":"mound","added":true}]},"notes":"'A single standing stone' for the menhir is our explanation (the document only shows photos). The dolmen diagram on page 16 is in Spanish; the English names are added. Page 14 has a map 'Megalitismo en el occidente europeo'.","vocabulary":[{"en":"megalithism","es":"megalitismo"},{"en":"megalith","es":"megalito"},{"en":"unworked stone","es":"piedra sin tallar","added":true},{"en":"menhir","es":"menhir"},{"en":"dolmen","es":"dolmen"},{"en":"cromlech","es":"crómlech"},{"en":"tomb","es":"tumba","added":true},{"en":"cult","es":"culto","added":true}],"dates":[{"when":"5th to 2nd millennium (BC)","what":"Megalithism"},{"when":"c. 4500 BC","what":"Grand Menhir Brisé erected (Brittany, France)"},{"when":"4th millennium (BC)","what":"Dolmen de Menga (Antequera, Málaga)"}]}],"timeline":[{"when":"12,000 BC","what":"wheat domesticated (Harari comic)"},{"when":"about 10,000 years ago","what":"beginning of the Neolithic: agriculture and livestock farming"},{"when":"7500 BC","what":"Fertile Crescent map"},{"when":"7th millennium BC","what":"metallurgy appears in the Near East"},{"when":"5th–2nd millennium BC","what":"Megalithism"},{"when":"5,000 BC","what":"Copper Age"},{"when":"c. 4500 BC","what":"Grand Menhir Brisé (Brittany)"},{"when":"4th millennium BC","what":"Dolmen de Menga"},{"when":"3,000 BC","what":"Bronze Age"},{"when":"2,000 BC","what":"Iron Age"}]},"study":{"topic":"prehistory","note":"Secciones de estudio y examen, en el orden del PDF (págs.). Los apartados pequeños se unen con el siguiente. 'sections' apunta a los apartados de prehistory.json; cada pregunta del examen pertenece a la sección que contiene su 'sec'. Los resúmenes son nuestros, hechos solo con lo que dice el documento.","cards":[{"id":"s1","ico":"🌾","pages":"1–2","title":"Paleolithic vs Neolithic: the Neolithic Revolution","sections":["neolithic-revolution"],"summary":["About 10,000 years ago, Homo sapiens sapiens learned to cultivate plants (agriculture) and tame animals (livestock farming). This is the big difference between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic.","Paleolithic = \"old stone\": people were nomadic, lived in caves and lived by hunting and gathering. Neolithic = \"new stone\".","It was a revolution: a change that transforms the world."],"images":[{"src":"farming","caption":"Neolithic people cultivating plants (agriculture)"},{"src":"herding","caption":"Taming animals: livestock farming"}]},{"id":"s2","ico":"🏘️","pages":"2–4","title":"Advantages and disadvantages of agriculture","sections":["advantages-of-agriculture","disadvantage-diseases"],"summary":["Advantages of agriculture: people become sedentary and build towns and cities (the first ones in the Fertile Crescent, like Çatalhöyük in Turkey); they grow their own food; the population increases (more food = more births).","Disadvantage: more people = more contagion. Living with animals, some animal diseases pass to humans (COVID, rabies, AIDS). Nomadic people without domesticated animals did not have these diseases."],"images":[{"src":"catalhoyuk","caption":"Çatalhöyük, one of the first towns, in present-day Turkey"},{"src":"fertile-crescent","caption":"The Fertile Crescent (7500 BC). Countries today: Turkey, Palestine, Israel, Iraq, Syria, Lebanon"}]},{"id":"s3","ico":"🐐","pages":"4–7","title":"Domesticated species and Harari","sections":["domesticated-species","harari"],"summary":["Of the thousands of species that ancient humans hunted and gathered, only a few could be domesticated. Learn at least 7 (with their dates).","Even today, half of the calories we eat come from 3 plants (wheat, rice, corn) and 2 animals (cows, pigs). All of them were domesticated more than 9,000 years ago.","Yuval Noah Harari (book \"Sapiens\") thinks agriculture was NOT a great advance: maybe humans lived better before, when they hunted and gathered.","The most successful species: 1 wheat (today it would cover all of Western Europe), 2 corn (American), 3 sugar cane (Asian), 4 rice (Asia), 5 potato (American, brought to Europe by the Spanish after Columbus)."],"images":[{"src":"comic-species-1","caption":"Harari's comic: domesticated species and their dates (−10.000 = 10,000 BC)"},{"src":"comic-species-2","caption":"More species: sheep, pig, cow, zebu, cat, llama, olive tree, wheat, pistachio, grapevine"},{"src":"harari","caption":"Yuval Noah Harari, author of \"Sapiens\""},{"src":"wheat-map","caption":"Wheat: its farmland covers more than 2 million km², almost all of Western Europe"},{"src":"comic-worse","caption":"\"My life as a farmer is much worse!\""},{"src":"comic-farmer","caption":"\"From sunrise to sunset he sowed the seeds, watered the plants, harvested and ground the grain… without rest!\""}]},{"id":"s4","ico":"👑","pages":"7–8","title":"Food shortages and social hierarchies","sections":["food-shortages","social-hierarchies"],"summary":["With more people there were sometimes food shortages, because agriculture depended completely on the climate: floods, drought and plagues of insects destroyed the crops.","With agriculture come the first cities and the first social hierarchies: kings and nobility, priests, scribes, craftsmen and peasants, and slaves at the bottom.","The first kings and elites appear by force and because they have the best lands, near the river: they produce more, sell more and have more power."],"images":[{"src":"locusts-egypt","caption":"A plague of locusts in ancient Egypt (according to the Bible)"},{"src":"pyramid-classes","caption":"The social pyramid"}]},{"id":"s5","ico":"🗿","pages":"9–10","title":"Barter and religion","sections":["barter","religion"],"summary":["The barter system is born: luxury products, like votive axes made of variscite, are exchanged.","Religion is born. The Venus of Willendorf was maybe a goddess of fertility. The first religions (fire, water, earth, wind) are called animist."],"images":[{"src":"votive-axes","caption":"Votive axes made of variscite: luxury products for barter"},{"src":"variscite","caption":"Variscite, a green stone"},{"src":"venus","caption":"The Venus of Willendorf: maybe a goddess of fertility"},{"src":"animism","caption":"Animist religions: nature (fire, water, earth, wind)"}]},{"id":"s6","ico":"🏺","pages":"10–11","title":"New tools, ceramics, basketry and textiles","sections":["new-tools","ceramics","basketry-and-textiles"],"summary":["New tools: the sickle, axes, the mill / mortar and the hoe.","Ceramics (pottery) are invented: 1 to preserve food, because now there are surpluses; 2 to avoid mice and plagues (like the hórreo in Asturias and Galicia); 3 to transport things.","Basketry, the loom and the spindle are also invented."],"images":[{"src":"sickle","caption":"Sickle (hoz)"},{"src":"mortar","caption":"Mortar (mortero)"},{"src":"hoe","caption":"Hoe (azada)"},{"src":"horreo","caption":"Hórreo: food is kept safe from mice"},{"src":"basketry","caption":"Basketry (cestería)"},{"src":"loom","caption":"Loom (telar) and spindle (huso)"},{"src":"spindle","caption":"Spindle (huso): used to spin textile fibres, from the Neolithic until today"}]},{"id":"s7","ico":"🌍","pages":"12","title":"Where does the Neolithic appear?","sections":["where-neolithic"],"summary":["The Neolithic appears in the Fertile Crescent, in Eastern Asia (valleys of the Indus and the Huang He or Yellow River) and in America: the central region (the Maya and Aztecs, many centuries later) and the Andean region (the Incas, many centuries later)."],"images":[{"src":"neolithic-map","caption":"Where the Neolithic appears"}]},{"id":"s8","ico":"⚒️","pages":"12–14","title":"The Age of Metals: copper, bronze and iron","sections":["age-of-metals","metals"],"summary":["Metallurgy (the art of making metals) appeared in the 7th millennium in the Near East (Palestine, Syria, Iraq…). A new era begins.","Timeline: Copper Age 5,000 → Bronze Age 3,000 → Iron Age 2,000 (BC).","Copper (Cu): good conductor of electricity, used in wires; its problem is that it is not very hard. Bronze: an alloy of copper and tin, harder than copper (the weapons in the Iliad and the Odyssey are bronze). Iron (Fe): harder and more resistant than bronze."],"images":[{"src":"copper-shield","caption":"A copper object"},{"src":"bronze-warrior","caption":"Bronze weapons, like in the Iliad"}]},{"id":"s9","ico":"🪨","pages":"14–17","title":"Megalithism: menhir, dolmen and cromlech","sections":["megalithism"],"summary":["Megalithism (5th to 2nd millennium): monuments made with large, unworked stones (mega = big, lithism = stone).","Three types: menhir (we don't know what it was for; maybe to mark a territory), dolmen (a tomb, like the Dolmen de Menga in Antequera, Málaga) and cromlech (stones in a circle, for a solar or agricultural cult; the most famous is Stonehenge)."],"images":[{"src":"megalith-map","caption":"Megalithism in Western Europe"},{"src":"menhir-row","caption":"Menhirs: the Grand Menhir Brisé (Brittany, France, c. 4500 BC)"},{"src":"dolmen-diagram","caption":"How a dolmen was built"},{"src":"menga","caption":"Dolmen de Menga (Antequera, Málaga), 4th millennium"},{"src":"stonehenge","caption":"Stonehenge, the most famous cromlech"}]}]},"bank":{"topic":"prehistory","note":"Banco de preguntas del examen (inglés, 1º ESO). Todas se responden con lo que dice el documento. Tipos: mc (tipo test; 'a' es la correcta y 'o' las incorrectas, se barajan), tf (verdadero/falso; 'fix' es la corrección), gap (hueco; 'a' = respuestas aceptadas), match (unir; se eligen 'n' parejas al azar del 'pool'), order (ordenar), spot (señalar en el mapa), open (abierta; la corrige el padre). Además, tools/build.py añade a cada sección una pregunta de unir vocabulario (vm-sN) con su vocabulario. 'img' = imagen (si es lista, se elige una al azar).","maps":{"neolithic":{"img":"neolithic-map-blank","w":1000,"h":550,"spots":[{"n":1,"id":"mesoamerica","label":"Central America (Mesoamerica): the Maya and Aztecs","x":20.5,"y":54.2,"bx":15.0,"by":60.0},{"n":2,"id":"andean","label":"The Andean region: the Incas","x":26.8,"y":71.3,"bx":20.5,"by":76.4},{"n":3,"id":"nile","label":"The Nile Valley (Egypt)","x":55.3,"y":50.0,"bx":52.0,"by":60.0},{"n":4,"id":"fertile","label":"The Fertile Crescent (Mesopotamia)","x":59.2,"y":46.9,"bx":58.5,"by":35.5},{"n":5,"id":"indus","label":"The Indus River valley","x":66.5,"y":50.5,"bx":68.0,"by":60.9},{"n":6,"id":"huanghe","label":"The Huang He (Yellow River) valley","x":78.0,"y":44.4,"bx":79.0,"by":33.6}]}},"questions":[{"id":"mc01","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"What is the difference between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic?","a":"Agriculture and livestock farming","o":["The use of fire","The use of metals","Writing"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc02","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"When did humans discover agriculture and livestock farming?","a":"About 10,000 years ago","o":["About 1,000 years ago","About 100,000 years ago","In 1789"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc03","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"What does \"Paleolithic\" mean?","a":"Old stone","o":["New stone","Big stone","Metal age"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc04","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"What does \"Neolithic\" mean?","a":"New stone","o":["Old stone","Big stone","First cities"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc05","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"How did people live in the Paleolithic?","a":"They were nomadic and lived by hunting and gathering","o":["They lived in big cities","They were farmers","They made bronze weapons"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc06","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"What is a revolution?","a":"A change that transforms the world","o":["A type of tool","A very old religion","A big stone monument"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"mc07","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Where were the first cities built?","a":"In the Fertile Crescent","o":["In Western Europe","In the Andean region","In Brittany, France"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc08","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Which of these countries is NOT in the Fertile Crescent today?","a":"Spain","o":["Iraq","Syria","Lebanon"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc09","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Which of these is an advantage of agriculture?","a":"People can be sedentary and build towns","o":["People have fewer babies","There are no more diseases","People go back to living in caves"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc10","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Why did the population increase in the Neolithic?","a":"More food = more births","o":["People lived in caves","There were no kings","Metals were invented"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc11","t":"mc","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Why did diseases increase in the Neolithic?","a":"More people and living with animals = more contagion","o":["People ate too much wheat","People were nomadic","Because of the barter system"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc12","t":"mc","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Which disease is transmitted by infected dogs?","a":"Rabies","o":["AIDS","COVID","Drought"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"mc13","t":"mc","sec":"harari","q":"According to Harari, what is the most successful domesticated species?","a":"Wheat","o":["Potato","Horse","Sugar cane"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"mc14","t":"mc","sec":"harari","q":"Who brought the potato to Europe?","a":"The Spanish, after Columbus","o":["The Egyptians","The Incas, by barter","Harari"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"mc15","t":"mc","sec":"harari","q":"Where do corn and the potato come from?","a":"America","o":["Asia","Europe","The Fertile Crescent"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"mc16","t":"mc","sec":"harari","q":"What does Yuval Noah Harari think about agriculture?","a":"It was NOT a great advance: maybe humans lived better before","o":["It was the best thing in history","It never existed","It started in the Iron Age"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"mc17","t":"mc","sec":"domesticated-species","q":"In Harari's comic, half of the calories we eat come from 3 plants and 2 animals. Which are the animals?","a":"Cows and pigs","o":["Horses and camels","Cats and hens","Goats and llamas"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"mc18","t":"mc","sec":"food-shortages","q":"Why were there sometimes food shortages?","a":"Agriculture depended completely on the climate","o":["People did not like bread","There were too many tools","Kings ate all the food"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc19","t":"mc","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"How did the first kings and elites arise?","a":"By force, and because they had the best lands","o":["By elections","By writing books","By building menhirs"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc20","t":"mc","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"Where were the best lands?","a":"Near the river","o":["On top of the mountains","In the desert","Inside the caves"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc21","t":"mc","sec":"barter","q":"Which luxury products were exchanged by barter?","a":"Votive axes made of variscite","o":["Gold coins","Iron swords","Potatoes"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"mc22","t":"mc","sec":"religion","q":"What are the first religions (fire, water, earth, wind) called?","a":"Animist","o":["Megalithic","Votive","Sedentary"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"mc23","t":"mc","sec":"ceramics","q":"Why were ceramics invented?","a":"To preserve food, because there were surpluses","o":["To make weapons","To build tombs","To mark a territory"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"mc24","t":"mc","sec":"ceramics","q":"What does \"surplus\" mean in Spanish?","a":"excedente","o":["escasez","sequía","trueque"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"mc25","t":"mc","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Where did the Neolithic appear in Eastern Asia?","a":"In the valleys of the Indus and the Huang He","o":["In the Andean region","In Antequera","In Brittany"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"mc26","t":"mc","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Which peoples lived in America many centuries later, where the Neolithic had appeared?","a":"The Maya, the Aztecs and the Incas","o":["The Romans and the Greeks","The Egyptians","The Spanish"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"mc27","t":"mc","sec":"age-of-metals","q":"When and where did metallurgy appear?","a":"In the 7th millennium, in the Near East","o":["In 1789, in France","In the 19th century, in factories","In Antequera, in the 4th millennium"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mc28","t":"mc","sec":"age-of-metals","q":"What is the correct order of the Age of Metals?","a":"Copper → Bronze → Iron","o":["Iron → Bronze → Copper","Bronze → Copper → Iron","Copper → Iron → Bronze"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mc29","t":"mc","sec":"metals","q":"What is bronze?","a":"An alloy of copper and tin","o":["An alloy of iron and copper","A type of stone","A pure metal like iron"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mc30","t":"mc","sec":"metals","q":"What is the main problem of copper?","a":"It is not very hard","o":["It does not conduct electricity","It is too heavy","Nobody wants it"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mc31","t":"mc","sec":"metals","q":"Which metal is the hardest and most resistant?","a":"Iron","o":["Copper","Bronze","Tin"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mc32","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","q":"What is Megalithism?","a":"Building monuments with large, unworked stones","o":["The art of making metals","A type of religion","Exchanging products"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"mc33","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","q":"What was a dolmen?","a":"A tomb","o":["A house","A tool","A circle of stones for the sun"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"mc34","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","q":"What is the most famous cromlech?","a":"Stonehenge","o":["Dolmen de Menga","Çatalhöyük","The Venus of Willendorf"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"mc35","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","q":"Where is the Dolmen de Menga?","a":"In Antequera (Málaga)","o":["In Brittany (France)","In Turkey","In England"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"mc36","t":"mc","sec":"metals","q":"Which Spanish word comes from the Latin \"ferrum\" (iron)?","a":"ferrocarril","o":["cobre","bronce","estaño"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"im01","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","img":"farming","q":"What are these people doing?","a":"Cultivating plants (agriculture)","o":["Hunting mammoths","Building a dolmen","Making bronze"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"im02","t":"mc","sec":"neolithic-revolution","img":"herding","q":"This man is taking care of his sheep. What is this activity called?","a":"Livestock farming","o":["Gathering","Metallurgy","Barter"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"im03","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","img":"catalhoyuk","q":"This is Çatalhöyük, one of the first towns. Where is it?","a":"In present-day Turkey","o":["In Spain","In China","In Peru"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"im04","t":"mc","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","img":"fertile-crescent","q":"What is the name of the green area on this map?","a":"The Fertile Crescent","o":["The Andean region","The Yellow River","Mesoamerica"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"im05","t":"mc","sec":"harari","img":"wheat-map","q":"In Harari's comic, which crop says: \"My farmland covers almost all of Western Europe\"?","a":"Wheat","o":["Corn","Rice","Potato"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"im06","t":"mc","sec":"harari","img":"comic-worse","q":"What does the farmer in this comic think?","a":"Her life as a farmer is worse than the life of a gatherer","o":["Farming is very easy","Hunting is boring","Wheat is her best friend"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"im07","t":"mc","sec":"religion","img":"venus","q":"What is this figure?","a":"The Venus of Willendorf, maybe a goddess of fertility","o":["A Roman queen","A menhir","A votive axe"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"im08","t":"mc","sec":"barter","img":"votive-axes","q":"These are votive axes made of variscite. What were they?","a":"Luxury products exchanged by barter","o":["Weapons made of iron","Tools to cultivate plants","Tombs"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"im09","t":"mc","sec":"new-tools","img":"sickle","q":"What is this tool?","a":"A sickle (hoz)","o":["A hoe (azada)","A mortar (mortero)","A spindle (huso)"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im10","t":"mc","sec":"new-tools","img":"mortar","q":"What is this tool?","a":"A mortar (mortero)","o":["A sickle (hoz)","A hoe (azada)","A loom (telar)"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im11","t":"mc","sec":"new-tools","img":"hoe","q":"What tool is this person using?","a":"A hoe (azada)","o":["A sickle (hoz)","A mortar (mortero)","A spindle (huso)"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im12","t":"mc","sec":"ceramics","img":"horreo","q":"This is a hórreo from Asturias or Galicia. What is it for?","a":"To keep food safe from mice and plagues","o":["To bury the dead","To mark a territory","To make metals"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im13","t":"mc","sec":"basketry-and-textiles","img":"basketry","q":"What is this man making?","a":"Basketry (cestería)","o":["Ceramics","Bronze","A dolmen"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im14","t":"mc","sec":"basketry-and-textiles","img":"loom","q":"What is this woman using to make fabric?","a":"A loom (telar)","o":["A sickle (hoz)","A hoe (azada)","A mortar (mortero)"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"im15","t":"mc","sec":"metals","img":"bronze-warrior","q":"In the Iliad and the Odyssey, what are all the weapons made of?","a":"Bronze","o":["Iron","Stone","Copper only"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"im16","t":"mc","sec":"food-shortages","img":"locusts-egypt","q":"What is destroying the crops in this picture?","a":"A plague of insects (locusts)","o":["A flood","A drought","An army"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"im17","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","img":["menhir-1","menhir-2","menhir-row"],"q":"What type of megalith is this?","a":"Menhir","o":["Dolmen","Cromlech"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"im18","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","img":["dolmen-1","dolmen-2","menga"],"q":"What type of megalith is this?","a":"Dolmen","o":["Menhir","Cromlech"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"im19","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","img":["stonehenge","stonehenge-air"],"q":"What type of megalith is this?","a":"Cromlech","o":["Menhir","Dolmen"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"im20","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","img":"dolmen-diagram","q":"This drawing shows how they built a…","a":"Dolmen (a tomb)","o":["Menhir","Cromlech","Hórreo"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"im21","t":"mc","sec":"megalithism","img":"menga-inside","q":"This is the inside of the Dolmen de Menga. What was it?","a":"A tomb","o":["A house","A temple for the sun","A market for barter"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"im22","t":"mc","sec":"domesticated-species","img":"comic-species-1","q":"Look at the comic. When was the horse (caballo) domesticated?","a":"5,500 BC","o":["10,000 BC","8,000 BC","5,000 BC"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"im23","t":"mc","sec":"domesticated-species","img":"comic-species-2","q":"Look at the comic. Which is the oldest domesticated species?","a":"Wheat (12,000 BC)","o":["Cat (8,000 BC)","Olive tree (6,500 BC)","Llama (5,000 BC)"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"im24","t":"mc","sec":"metals","img":"copper-shield","q":"This object is made of copper. What is copper good at?","a":"It is a good conductor of electricity","o":["It is very, very hard","It is harder than iron","It is an alloy of tin"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"sp01","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the Fertile Crescent.","a":4,"g":"s7"},{"id":"sp02","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the Indus River valley.","a":5,"g":"s7"},{"id":"sp03","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the Huang He (Yellow River) valley.","a":6,"g":"s7"},{"id":"sp04","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the central region of America (later, the Maya and the Aztecs).","a":1,"g":"s7"},{"id":"sp05","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the Andean region (later, the Incas).","a":2,"g":"s7"},{"id":"sp06","t":"spot","sec":"where-neolithic","map":"neolithic","q":"Tap the Nile Valley (Egypt).","a":3,"g":"s7"},{"id":"or01","t":"order","sec":"social-hierarchies","img":"pyramid-people","q":"Put the social pyramid in order, from the TOP to the BOTTOM.","a":["Kings and Nobility","Priests","Scribes","Craftsmen and Peasants","Slaves"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"or02","t":"order","sec":"age-of-metals","img":"copper-shield","q":"Put the Age of Metals in order, from the OLDEST to the MOST RECENT.","a":["Copper Age","Bronze Age","Iron Age"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"or03","t":"order","sec":"metals","q":"Order the metals from the SOFTEST to the HARDEST.","a":["Copper","Bronze","Iron"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"or04","t":"order","sec":"ceramics","img":"mouse","q":"Remember the 3 reasons why ceramics were invented, in the order of the unit.","a":["To preserve food (surpluses)","To avoid mice and plagues","For transporting things"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"tf01","t":"tf","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"In the Paleolithic, people were nomadic.","a":true,"g":"s1"},{"id":"tf02","t":"tf","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"\"Neolithic\" means \"old stone\".","a":false,"fix":"Neolithic means \"new stone\". Paleolithic means \"old stone\".","g":"s1"},{"id":"tf03","t":"tf","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Agriculture allowed people to be sedentary.","a":true,"g":"s2"},{"id":"tf04","t":"tf","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Sedentary means the same as nomadic.","a":false,"fix":"Sedentary is the opposite of nomadic.","g":"s2"},{"id":"tf05","t":"tf","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"The first cities were built in the Fertile Crescent.","a":true,"g":"s2"},{"id":"tf06","t":"tf","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Egypt is one of the countries of the Fertile Crescent in the unit's list.","a":false,"fix":"The list is: Turkey, Palestine, Israel, Iraq, Syria and Lebanon.","g":"s2"},{"id":"tf07","t":"tf","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"With agriculture, more food meant more births.","a":true,"g":"s2"},{"id":"tf08","t":"tf","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Agriculture reduced diseases.","a":false,"fix":"More people = more contagion, and some animal diseases passed to humans.","g":"s2"},{"id":"tf09","t":"tf","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Nomadic humans without domesticated animals did not have these animal diseases.","a":true,"g":"s2"},{"id":"tf10","t":"tf","sec":"domesticated-species","q":"Ancient humans could domesticate almost all the species they hunted.","a":false,"fix":"Only a few species were domesticable.","g":"s3"},{"id":"tf11","t":"tf","sec":"harari","q":"According to Harari, maybe humans lived better before agriculture.","a":true,"g":"s3"},{"id":"tf12","t":"tf","sec":"harari","q":"The potato was brought to Europe by the Spanish after Columbus.","a":true,"g":"s3"},{"id":"tf13","t":"tf","sec":"harari","q":"Rice comes from America.","a":false,"fix":"Rice comes from Asia. Corn and the potato are American.","g":"s3"},{"id":"tf14","t":"tf","sec":"food-shortages","q":"Floods, drought and plagues could destroy the crops.","a":true,"g":"s4"},{"id":"tf15","t":"tf","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"Kings and elites had the best lands, near the river.","a":true,"g":"s4"},{"id":"tf16","t":"tf","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"Slaves were at the top of the social pyramid.","a":false,"fix":"Kings and nobility were at the top; slaves were at the bottom.","g":"s4"},{"id":"tf17","t":"tf","sec":"barter","q":"The barter system was born with agriculture and livestock farming.","a":true,"g":"s5"},{"id":"tf18","t":"tf","sec":"religion","q":"The first religions were called animist.","a":true,"g":"s5"},{"id":"tf19","t":"tf","sec":"ceramics","q":"Ceramics were invented to preserve food.","a":true,"g":"s6"},{"id":"tf20","t":"tf","sec":"basketry-and-textiles","q":"The loom and the spindle were invented in the Iron Age.","a":false,"fix":"They were invented in the Neolithic.","g":"s6"},{"id":"tf21","t":"tf","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"The Neolithic only appeared in the Fertile Crescent.","a":false,"fix":"It also appeared in Eastern Asia (Indus, Huang He) and in America.","g":"s7"},{"id":"tf22","t":"tf","sec":"age-of-metals","q":"Metallurgy appeared in the Near East.","a":true,"g":"s8"},{"id":"tf23","t":"tf","sec":"metals","q":"Bronze is an alloy of copper and iron.","a":false,"fix":"Bronze is an alloy of copper and TIN.","g":"s8"},{"id":"tf24","t":"tf","sec":"metals","q":"Iron is softer than bronze.","a":false,"fix":"Iron is harder and more resistant than bronze.","g":"s8"},{"id":"tf25","t":"tf","sec":"metals","q":"Copper is a good conductor of electricity.","a":true,"g":"s8"},{"id":"tf26","t":"tf","sec":"megalithism","q":"A dolmen was a tomb.","a":true,"g":"s9"},{"id":"tf27","t":"tf","sec":"megalithism","q":"Stonehenge is a menhir.","a":false,"fix":"Stonehenge is a cromlech (megaliths in a circle).","g":"s9"},{"id":"tf28","t":"tf","sec":"megalithism","q":"Megaliths were built with small, worked stones.","a":false,"fix":"They were built with LARGE, UNWORKED stones.","g":"s9"},{"id":"tf29","t":"tf","sec":"megalithism","q":"We know for sure what menhirs were for.","a":false,"fix":"We don't know for sure. Maybe to mark a territory.","g":"s9"},{"id":"tf30","t":"tf","sec":"megalithism","q":"A cromlech has to do with a solar or agricultural cult.","a":true,"g":"s9"},{"id":"gp01","t":"gap","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"In the Paleolithic, people lived by hunting and ___.","a":["gathering"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"gp02","t":"gap","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"\"Neolithic\" means new ___.","a":["stone"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"gp03","t":"gap","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"Taming animals is called livestock ___.","a":["farming"],"g":"s1"},{"id":"gp04","t":"gap","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Sedentary is the opposite of ___.","a":["nomadic"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp05","t":"gap","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"The first cities were built in the Fertile ___.","a":["crescent"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp06","t":"gap","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Çatalhöyük is in present-day ___.","a":["turkey"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp07","t":"gap","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"More food = more ___.","a":["births","babies"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp08","t":"gap","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"More people = more ___.","a":["contagion"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp09","t":"gap","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Rabies is transmitted by infected ___.","a":["dogs","dog"],"g":"s2"},{"id":"gp10","t":"gap","sec":"harari","q":"According to Harari, the most successful domesticated species is ___.","a":["wheat"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"gp11","t":"gap","sec":"harari","q":"The potato was brought to Europe by the ___ after Columbus.","a":["spanish","spaniards"],"g":"s3"},{"id":"gp12","t":"gap","sec":"food-shortages","q":"Floods, drought and plagues caused food ___.","a":["shortages","shortage"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"gp13","t":"gap","sec":"barter","q":"With agriculture and livestock farming, the ___ system is born.","a":["barter"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"gp14","t":"gap","sec":"religion","q":"The first religions are called ___.","a":["animist"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"gp15","t":"gap","sec":"ceramics","q":"Ceramics are also called ___.","a":["pottery"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"gp16","t":"gap","sec":"ceramics","q":"Ceramics were used to preserve food because now there were ___.","a":["surpluses","surplus"],"g":"s6"},{"id":"gp17","t":"gap","sec":"metals","q":"Bronze is an alloy of copper and ___.","a":["tin"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"gp18","t":"gap","sec":"metals","q":"Iron is harder and more resistant than ___.","a":["bronze"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"gp19","t":"gap","sec":"megalithism","q":"A dolmen was a ___.","a":["tomb"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"gp20","t":"gap","sec":"megalithism","q":"A cromlech is a group of megaliths in the form of a ___.","a":["circle"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"gp21","t":"gap","sec":"megalithism","q":"The most famous cromlech is ___.","a":["stonehenge"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"gp22","t":"gap","sec":"megalithism","q":"In \"megalith\", MEGA means ___.","a":["big"],"g":"s9"},{"id":"gp23","t":"gap","sec":"age-of-metals","q":"Metallurgy appeared in the Near ___.","a":["east"],"g":"s8"},{"id":"gp24","t":"gap","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"The Huang He is also called the ___ River.","a":["yellow"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"mt01","t":"match","sec":"age-of-metals","q":"Match each period of the Age of Metals with its date.","n":4,"pool":[["Copper Age","5,000 BC"],["Bronze Age","3,000 BC"],["Iron Age","2,000 BC"],["Metallurgy appears","7th millennium BC"]],"g":"s8"},{"id":"mt02","t":"match","sec":"megalithism","q":"Match each megalith word with its meaning.","n":4,"pool":[["Menhir","Maybe it marked a territory"],["Dolmen","A tomb"],["Cromlech","Stones in a circle"],["Megalithism","Monuments with large, unworked stones"],["Stonehenge","The most famous cromlech"],["Dolmen de Menga","In Antequera (Málaga)"]],"g":"s9"},{"id":"mt03","t":"match","sec":"domesticated-species","q":"Match each species with the date it was domesticated (Harari's comic).","n":4,"pool":[["Wheat","12,000 BC"],["Sheep","10,500 BC"],["Goat","10,000 BC"],["Zebu","8,500 BC"],["Cat","8,000 BC"],["Olive tree","6,500 BC"],["Horse","5,500 BC"],["Camel","5,000 BC"]],"g":"s3"},{"id":"op01","t":"open","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"What is the difference between the Paleolithic and the Neolithic? Explain it.","a":"Agriculture and livestock farming. In the Paleolithic people were nomadic, lived in caves and lived by hunting and gathering. In the Neolithic (about 10,000 years ago) they cultivated plants and tamed animals.","g":"s1"},{"id":"op02","t":"open","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","q":"Say three advantages of agriculture.","a":"1. People become sedentary and build towns and cities. 2. Humans grow their own food. 3. The population increases (more food = more births).","g":"s2"},{"id":"op03","t":"open","sec":"disadvantage-diseases","q":"Why did diseases increase in the Neolithic? Give an example.","a":"More people = more contagion. Humans live with animals and some animal diseases pass to humans: COVID (bats?), rabies (dogs), AIDS.","g":"s2"},{"id":"op04","t":"open","sec":"domesticated-species","q":"Say at least 7 domesticated species.","a":"Any 7 of: wheat, peas and lentils, goat, sheep, pig, cow, zebu, hen, cat, pistachio, grapevine, olive tree, horse, camel, llama (also corn, rice, sugar cane, potato).","g":"s3"},{"id":"op05","t":"open","sec":"harari","q":"What does Harari think about agriculture? Which are the most successful species?","a":"Agriculture was NOT a great advance; maybe humans lived better before, when they hunted and gathered. Most successful: wheat, corn, sugar cane, rice, potato.","g":"s3"},{"id":"op06","t":"open","sec":"food-shortages","q":"Why were there food shortages in the Neolithic?","a":"There were more people, and agriculture depended completely on the climate: floods, drought and plagues destroyed the crops.","g":"s4"},{"id":"op07","t":"open","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"How did the first kings and elites appear?","a":"By force, and because they had the best lands (near the river): they produced more, sold more, obtained more and had more power.","g":"s4"},{"id":"op08","t":"open","sec":"ceramics","q":"Why were ceramics invented? Say three reasons.","a":"1. To preserve food, because there were surpluses. 2. To avoid mice and plagues. 3. For transporting things.","g":"s6"},{"id":"op09","t":"open","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Where did the Neolithic appear?","a":"In the Fertile Crescent; in Eastern Asia (valleys of the Indus and the Huang He / Yellow River); and in America (the central region and the Andean region).","g":"s7"},{"id":"op10","t":"open","sec":"metals","q":"Explain the three metals of the Age of Metals.","a":"Copper: good conductor of electricity, but not very hard. Bronze: alloy of copper and tin, harder than copper. Iron: harder and more resistant than bronze.","g":"s8"},{"id":"op11","t":"open","sec":"megalithism","q":"Name and explain the three types of megaliths.","a":"Menhir: we don't know what it was for, maybe to mark a territory. Dolmen: a tomb (Dolmen de Menga). Cromlech: stones in a circle, for a solar or agricultural cult (Stonehenge).","g":"s9"},{"id":"op12","t":"open","sec":"religion","q":"How was religion in the Neolithic?","a":"Religion is born. The first religions are very primitive (fire, water, earth, wind) and are called animist. The Venus of Willendorf was maybe a goddess of fertility.","g":"s5"},{"id":"tf31","t":"tf","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"In the Paleolithic, people lived in caves.","a":true,"g":"s1"},{"id":"tf32","t":"tf","sec":"neolithic-revolution","q":"Historians talk about the Neolithic Revolution.","a":true,"g":"s1"},{"id":"tf33","t":"tf","sec":"food-shortages","q":"At that time, agriculture depended completely on the climate.","a":true,"g":"s4"},{"id":"gp25","t":"gap","sec":"food-shortages","q":"Floods, ___ and plagues destroyed the crops.","a":["drought"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc37","t":"mc","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"What is a hierarchy (jerarquía)?","a":"A gradation of people, values or dignities","o":["A type of tool","A plague of insects","A tomb"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc38","t":"mc","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"Who is at the bottom of the social pyramid?","a":"Slaves","o":["Priests","Scribes","Kings and Nobility"],"g":"s4"},{"id":"tf34","t":"tf","sec":"social-hierarchies","q":"In the social pyramid, priests are above scribes.","a":true,"g":"s4"},{"id":"mc39","t":"mc","sec":"barter","q":"What is the Spanish word for \"barter\"?","a":"trueque","o":["excedente","escasez","telar"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"gp26","t":"gap","sec":"barter","q":"Votive axes were made of ___.","a":["variscite"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"im25","t":"mc","sec":"barter","img":"variscite","q":"Votive axes were made of this green stone. What is it called?","a":"Variscite","o":["Copper","Bronze","Tin"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"tf35","t":"tf","sec":"religion","q":"The Venus of Willendorf was maybe a goddess of fertility.","a":true,"g":"s5"},{"id":"tf36","t":"tf","sec":"religion","q":"The first religions were very modern and complex.","a":false,"fix":"The first religions were very primitive (fire, water, earth, wind).","g":"s5"},{"id":"mc40","t":"mc","sec":"religion","q":"What did the first religions worship?","a":"Fire, water, earth and wind","o":["Metals","Kings","Wheat and rice"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"im26","t":"mc","sec":"religion","img":"animism","q":"The first religions worshipped nature (fire, water, earth, wind). What are they called?","a":"Animist","o":["Megalithic","Votive","Sedentary"],"g":"s5"},{"id":"tf37","t":"tf","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"In America, the Neolithic appeared in the central region and in the Andean region.","a":true,"g":"s7"},{"id":"tf38","t":"tf","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"The Huang He is also called the Yellow River.","a":true,"g":"s7"},{"id":"mc41","t":"mc","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Many centuries later, in which region did the Incas live?","a":"The Andean region","o":["The Fertile Crescent","Eastern Asia","The Near East"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"mc42","t":"mc","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Many centuries later, who lived in the central region of America?","a":"The Maya and the Aztecs","o":["The Incas","The Egyptians","The Spanish"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"gp27","t":"gap","sec":"where-neolithic","q":"Many centuries later, the Incas lived in the ___ region.","a":["andean"],"g":"s7"},{"id":"vm-s1","t":"match","sec":"neolithic-revolution","g":"s1","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["Paleolithic","Paleolítico (edad de la piedra antigua)"],["Neolithic","Neolítico (edad de la piedra nueva)"],["agriculture","agricultura"],["livestock farming","ganadería"],["nomadic","nómada"],["hunting and gathering","caza y recolección"],["revolution","revolución"]]},{"id":"vm-s2","t":"match","sec":"advantages-of-agriculture","g":"s2","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["sedentary","sedentario"],["Fertile Crescent","Creciente Fértil"],["population","población"],["contagion","contagio"],["rabies","rabia"],["AIDS","SIDA"]]},{"id":"vm-s3","t":"match","sec":"domesticated-species","g":"s3","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["to domesticate","domesticar"],["wheat","trigo"],["goat","cabra"],["sheep","oveja"],["horse","caballo"],["olive tree","olivo"],["grapevine","parra"],["corn","maíz"],["sugar cane","caña de azúcar"],["to gather","recolectar"]]},{"id":"vm-s4","t":"match","sec":"food-shortages","g":"s4","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["food shortage","escasez de alimentos"],["flood","inundación"],["drought","sequía"],["plague","plaga (de insectos)"],["social hierarchy","jerarquía social"]]},{"id":"vm-s5","t":"match","sec":"barter","g":"s5","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["barter","trueque"],["votive","votivo (ofrecido por voto o promesa)"],["religion","religión"],["goddess of fertility","diosa de la fertilidad"],["animist","animista"]]},{"id":"vm-s6","t":"match","sec":"new-tools","g":"s6","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["sickle","hoz"],["mortar","mortero"],["hoe","azada"],["ceramics / pottery","cerámica"],["surplus","excedente"],["hórreo","hórreo (granero elevado asturiano o gallego)"],["basketry","cestería"],["loom","telar"],["spindle","huso"]]},{"id":"vm-s7","t":"match","sec":"where-neolithic","g":"s7","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":4,"pool":[["Yellow River (Huang He)","río Amarillo"],["Andean region","zona de los Andes"],["valley","valle"],["river","río"]]},{"id":"vm-s8","t":"match","sec":"age-of-metals","g":"s8","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["metallurgy","metalurgia"],["Near East","Próximo Oriente"],["timeline","eje cronológico"],["copper","cobre"],["tin","estaño"],["alloy","aleación"]]},{"id":"vm-s9","t":"match","sec":"megalithism","g":"s9","q":"Match the English word with its Spanish meaning.","n":5,"pool":[["megalithism","megalitismo"],["megalith","megalito"],["menhir","menhir"],["dolmen","dolmen"],["cromlech","crómlech"]]}]}};
